##  Course Guideline: Paper Critique

As part of this course you are required to critically assess a scientific paper selected from the provided list (at this point every student is assigned a paper). The critique has **two components**:

- **Written Critique** (10 % of final mark)
- **Oral Presentation** (20 % of final mark)

Both components assess your ability to read, evaluate, and communicate scientific work clearly and thoughtfully.

---

### Written Critique

- **Task:** Provide a written assessment of your assigned scientific paper.
- **Length:** Concise but comprehensive (typically 3–4 pages).
- **Due Date:** End of week 10.
- **Value:** 20 % of final mark.

#### Structure & Expectations
Your written critique should include the following. Both me and the peer that will grade your work will be looking for the following:

- **Brief summary (1 mark)**
  A very short synopsis of the paper’s aims, methods, and findings.

- **Understanding (2 marks)**
  Explain what you understood clearly and what you did not understand. Be specific about difficult concepts, figures, or methods.

- **Strengths & Weaknesses (3 marks)**
  Identify what was done well (e.g. clear methods, robust results) and what was done poorly (e.g. unclear assumptions, missing data).

- **Scientific Reception (2 marks)**
  - Provide citation counts (Scopus, Google Scholar, Web of Science).
  - Give examples of how and why the paper has been cited by others.

- **Impact (2 marks)**
  Discuss whether the paper influenced the field. Support with evidence and examples.

**Additional requirement for Honours/Masters students (5 marks, the mark ratios will be adjusted accordingly):**
Include a detailed critique of **two subsequent papers that cited your chosen article**, addressing:
- Why the citation was made,
- How the original article influenced the subsequent work,
- Whether the later authors captured the key contributions of the original.

---

### Oral Critique (Presentation)

- **Task:** Present an oral critique of the same paper.
- **Format:** 10 minutes presentation + 5 minutes questions.
- **Due Date:** Presentations commence in Week 9.
- **Value:** 10 % of final mark.

#### Structure & Expectations
Your oral presentation should cover the same core areas as the written critique:

- **Understanding (2 marks)**
  Explain what you did and did not understand, and why.

- **Strengths & Weaknesses (2 marks)**
  Describe what was done well and poorly in the paper, with justification.

- **Scientific Reception (2 marks)**
  Discuss how the scientific community treated the paper after publication.

- **Impact (1 mark)**
  Describe the impact—or lack thereof—on the field.

- **Presentation Quality (3 marks total)**
  - Delivery: clarity, engagement, confidence (1.5 marks)
  - Slides: clarity, appropriateness, effectiveness (1.5 marks)

---

### Peer Assessment

An important part of this assignment is that **you will grade each other’s work**.
This has two purposes:
1. To help you engage critically with the work of your peers,
2. To develop your ability to evaluate scientific communication against clear criteria.

#### How it works
- For both the **Written Critique** and the **Oral Critique**, each student will be assigned one peer’s work to evaluate.
- You must complete the assessment using the same marking rubric provided above.
- Your scores and comments will directly contribute to your peer’s final grade.

#### How grades are determined
- Your **own grade** will be made up of:
  - **50%** from the mark assigned by your peer,
  - **50%** from my assessment of your written report.
- I will review all peer assessments to ensure fairness.
- If there is a **large discrepancy** between peer and my judgement, I reserve the right to **veto** and adjust the grade. This will naturally affect your own grade.

This means your responsibility is twofold:
1. Deliver a thoughtful written and oral critique of your assigned paper.
2. Provide a fair, well-reasoned evaluation of a peer’s critique.

---

## Tips for Success

- **For both written and oral critiques:**
  - Focus on *analysis*, not just summary.
  - Provide evidence (citations, examples, data) where possible.
  - Distinguish clearly between your own perspective and what is shown in the paper.

- **For oral presentations:**
  - Engage with your audience (eye contact, clear voice, avoid reading directly).
  - Use visuals to highlight key points (not to overload with text).
  - Keep within the time limit.
  - Make sure figures and text on slides are legible.

- **For peer marking:**
  - Be constructive and respectful.
  - Justify your scores clearly.
  - Highlight strengths as well as areas for improvement.

